#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d65fc7-222b-57df-af18-d5f179328795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374081} {:answer/id #uuid "00c9eb58-0f77-54ed-b99a-75d7ea415170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374082} {:answer/id #uuid "72547652-05c5-564c-9e68-1ce3e6081bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374083} {:answer/id #uuid "52bff97b-d237-5101-b351-f70e38d15ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374084} {:answer/id #uuid "c2033edc-46d0-5266-ab71-615b97de26c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00c9eb58-0f77-54ed-b99a-75d7ea415170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374082} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca1d5cf-effe-5489-9ee3-4eb786f87d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9642dfed-e959-50ee-bb3a-bb0e4c6f718b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335252" :question/problem "For which of the following functions does the continuous-partial-derivative sufficient theorem apply throughout the stated parameter domain? Apply the theorem locally on a rectangle $[0,1]\\times[c,d]$: the integrand and its partial derivative with respect to $t$ must both be continuous there, and the partial derivative must exist at every point of the rectangle.\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "The stated sufficient theorem applies to I and III. For I, the partial derivative is $-2txe^{-t^2x}$; for III, it is $-2t/(x^2+t^2)^2$. Both integrands and their partial derivatives are continuous on a closed rectangle around every allowed parameter value, taking $c>0$ for III.\n\nFor II, $\\partial_t|x-t|$ equals $-1$ when $x>t$ and $1$ when $x<t$, but does not exist at $x=t$. Thus the stated theorem does not apply throughout its parameter domain. Its failure does not prove that interchange is invalid: the difference quotients are bounded in absolute value by $1$ and converge for almost every $x$, so dominated convergence justifies differentiation under the integral for II. Explicitly, $F_2(t)=t^2-t+1/2$ for $0\\le t\\le1$ and $F_2(t)=t-1/2$ for $t\\ge1$, giving $F_2′(t)=2t-1$ on $(0,1)$ and $1$ for $t\\ge1$, with right derivative $-1$ at $0$.\n\nUnder the explicit sufficient-theorem request, the answer is I and III only."}]}
